course: dictionary
lesson: 8219
title: Reuse, not throw away
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Reuse is made from two parts: re- means again, and use means to do a job with something. So to reuse something is to use it again instead of throwing it away. «Old phones are taken apart, their batteries removed safely and their metals melted out and reused.» Here reused means used again, for a new job.
explain: Reuse can also be a noun, which means the act of using things again. Look at the words around it to tell which one it is. «We can help by the three Rs: Reduce (use less), Reuse (use things again) and Recycle (turn old materials into new things).» The brackets explain each R, so you can see what reuse means.
explain: Reuse is easy to mix up with recycling, but they are not the same. Reuse keeps a thing as it is and uses it again. Recycling breaks a thing down to make new material. «The order is reduce first, reuse second and recycle third, because recycling still uses energy.»
fact: To reuse something means to use it again instead of throwing it away.
fact: Reuse can be a noun too: «reuse» is the act of using things again.
fact: The three Rs are reduce, reuse and recycle, and they are three ways to make less waste.
fact: «A reusable bottle can replace hundreds of throwaway ones.»
fact: Reusing saves materials and energy, and it makes less rubbish.
exercise: «Old phones are taken apart, their batteries removed safely and their metals melted out and reused.» What does reused mean here? | Thrown in the household bin | *Used again for a new job | Lost forever | Painted a bright colour
exercise: Which of these is an example of reuse? | Buying a new bottle every day | Throwing a plastic bag away after one shop | *Using a glass jar to store buttons | Burning old paper
exercise: «The order is reduce first, reuse second and recycle third.» Which of the three Rs comes second? | *Reuse | Reduce | Recycle | Waste
test: What is the difference between reuse and recycling? | Recycling keeps a thing as it is | *Reuse uses a thing again as it is, while recycling breaks it down to make new material | They mean exactly the same thing | Reuse breaks a thing down to make new material
test: Which of these is a reusable thing? | A paper plate for one picnic | *A refillable water bottle | A crisp packet | A plastic straw used once
exam: «Reduce, Reuse and Recycle are three ways to make less waste.» Which order of the three Rs is correct? | Recycle, reduce, reuse | Recycle, reuse, reduce | Reuse, recycle, reduce | *Reduce, reuse, recycle
